  urldate = {2024-08-01},
  abstract = {Neo-Kantianism was the dominant philosophical movement in Germany fromroughly 1870 until the First World War. This movement drew inspirationfrom a diverse cast of philosophers—principally, Kuno Fischer(Fischer 1860), Hermann von Helmholtz (Helmholtz 1867, 1878),Friedrich Lange (Lange 1866), Otto Liebmann (Liebmann 1865), andEduard Zeller (Zeller 1862))—who in the middle of the nineteenthcentury were calling for a return to Kant’s philosophy as analternative to both speculative metaphysics and materialism (Beiser2014b). During the 1870s, the movement formed into two schools, onebased around Hermann Cohen at Marburg University and another based insouthwest Germany (in the province of Baden) around WilhelmWindelband. Later members of the Marburg School include Paul Natorpand Ernst Cassirer; later members of the Southwest School includeHeinrich Rickert and Emil Lask.[1]},
